      "section_type": "content",
      "id": 5,
      "title": "Physics: Wave Mechanics",
      "renderer": "manim",
      "narration": "Let's now ride the waves into physics. Waves are fascinating phenomena that transfer energy from one place to another without transferring matter. Think of a ripple in a pond; the water molecules mostly move up and down, but the wave itself travels outwards. To describe any wave, whether it's sound, light, or water, we use a few key properties. First is the wavelength, represented by the Greek letter lambda (λ). This is the spatial period of the wave—the distance over which the wave's shape repeats. You can measure it from one crest to the next, or one trough to the next. Next is the frequency, denoted by 'f'. This tells us how many complete wave cycles pass a fixed point per second. Its unit is Hertz (Hz). A high-frequency wave has crests passing by very rapidly. These properties are linked together by the wave's speed, 'v', in the fundamental wave equation: v equals f times lambda. This makes perfect sense: if you multiply the number of waves passing per second (frequency) by the length of each wave (wavelength), you get the total distance the wave front travels per second (speed). Finally, there's the period, 'T', which is the time it takes for one single, complete wave cycle to pass a point. It's the inverse of frequency. If the frequency is 10 waves per second, the period is one-tenth of a second per wave. So, T equals 1 over f. These simple relationships are the bedrock of wave mechanics.",
