      "title": "Chemistry: The Equilibrium Constant (Kc)",
      "renderer": "manim",
      "narration": "From the physical world of waves, we now transition to the molecular interactions of chemistry, specifically focusing on reversible reactions and equilibrium. Unlike some reactions that proceed in one direction until a reactant is used up, many reactions are reversible. This means that as products are formed, they can also react with each other to re-form the original reactants. Let's consider a general reaction where 'a' moles of reactant A and 'b' moles of reactant B react to form 'c' moles of product C and 'd' moles of product D. Initially, the forward reaction is fast, but as products C and D build up, the reverse reaction begins to speed up. Eventually, the reaction reaches a state of dynamic equilibrium. This doesn't mean the reaction has stopped! It means the rate of the forward reaction perfectly matches the rate of the reverse reaction. Molecules are still constantly reacting, but the overall concentrations of reactants and products remain constant. To quantify this state, we use the equilibrium constant, Kc. The formula for Kc is a ratio: the concentrations of the products, raised to the power of their stoichiometric coefficients, are placed in the numerator, while the concentrations of the reactants, also raised to their coefficients, are in the denominator. So, Kc equals [C] to the power of c times [D] to the power of d, all divided by [A] to the power of a times [B] to the power of b. A large Kc value means products are heavily favored at equilibrium, while a small Kc value means the reaction mixture consists mostly of reactants.",
