    {
      "section_type": "content",
      "id": 7,
      "title": "Biology: The Endosymbiotic Theory",
      "renderer": "wan_video",
      "narration": "Our final topic takes us deep into the past to answer a fundamental question in biology: how did complex eukaryotic cells, the building blocks of animals, plants, and fungi, evolve from simpler prokaryotic cells? The leading explanation is the Endosymbiotic Theory. This theory proposes a remarkable story of ancient partnership. It begins over a billion years ago with a large, ancestral host cell, which was a prokaryote itself. This host cell engulfed smaller, free-living prokaryotes. Instead of being digested for food, some of these engulfed cells survived and thrived inside the host. A crucial event occurred when the host cell engulfed an aerobic bacterium, one that was highly efficient at using oxygen to produce energy in the form of ATP. This bacterium formed a symbiotic relationship with its host: it supplied abundant energy, and in return, the host cell provided a safe environment and nutrients. Over millions of years of co-evolution, this bacterium lost its independence and became the organelle we now know as the mitochondrion, the powerhouse of the cell. A similar event is proposed for the origin of chloroplasts. A host cell that already contained mitochondria engulfed a photosynthetic bacterium, a cyanobacterium. This bacterium could harness sunlight to create food. This partnership gave the host a new ability: making its own food. This engulfed bacterium evolved into the chloroplast, the site of photosynthesis in all plant and algae cells. Strong evidence supports this theory, including the fact that both mitochondria and chloroplasts have their own circular DNA, similar to bacteria, and their own ribosomes, and they replicate independently of the main cell.",
